      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What a title tag does on a practice website"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "The title tag is the line of text in your page's HTML that tells browsers and search engines what the page is called. It appears on the browser tab, and Google often uses it as the blue headline in search results. For a patient scanning ten results on a phone, it is the first thing they read about your practice and frequently the only thing."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Google calls this a title link and builds it mainly from your title element, then your visible headings and other signals. Google may rewrite a title it thinks is too long, empty, repeated across pages, or not a good match for the page. Writing a clear title yourself keeps more of that control. The details are in Google's [guidance on title links](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/title-link)."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "The pattern that works for most pages"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Lead with the thing the page is about, add the place or provider if it helps a patient choose, and finish with the practice name. Your most important words should come first because long titles get cut off at the end, and the cut-off point depends on pixel width rather than a fixed number of characters. A common rule of thumb is to keep titles to roughly 50 to 60 characters. Treat that as a working guide, not a Google rule."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Page type",
          "Pattern and example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Home page",
            "Practice type + city | Practice name. Example: Family Medicine in Wellington, FL | Example Medical"
          ],
          [
            "Service page",
            "Service + city | Practice name. Example: Sports Physicals in Wellington | Example Medical"
          ],
          [
            "Provider page",
            "Provider name, credential + specialty | Practice name. Example: Jane Rivera, MD, Internal Medicine | Example Medical"
          ],
          [
            "Location page",
            "Practice type + neighborhood or city | Practice name. Example: Urgent Care in Boynton Beach | Example Medical"
          ],
          [
            "Blog or guide",
            "The question or topic in plain words. Example: How to Prepare for Your First Visit | Example Medical"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "The practice and provider names above are placeholders. Use your own exact legal or public-facing names, spelled the same way as on your Google Business Profile."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Write one title per page, not one title per template"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Duplicate titles are the most common problem we see on practice sites. A website platform often fills every page with the same default, such as the practice name alone or the page name plus the same suffix. Google then has no way to tell pages apart in a list. If your sixty pages share three titles, you are asking Google to guess."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Give each page its own title based on the one question it answers. If two pages end up with nearly identical titles, that usually means they cover the same topic and one of them should be merged or retargeted. Our guide on [writing a service page that ranks](/guides/how-to-write-a-medical-service-page-that-ranks) covers how to choose one intent per page."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What to leave out"
      },
      {
        "type": "ul",
        "items": [
          "Keyword lists. A title such as Doctor, Physician, Clinic, Primary Care, Urgent Care Wellington reads as spam to patients and tends to get rewritten.",
          "The same phrase on every page. Repeating your city and specialty in all sixty titles makes them blur together.",
          "Claims you cannot support. Avoid best, top-rated, number one, guaranteed or outcome language. For healthcare, claims that sound clinical or promise results can also create compliance trouble.",
          "ALL CAPS and symbol strings. They are harder to read and add nothing.",
          "A title that disagrees with the page. If the title promises same-day appointments and the page does not mention them, patients leave and Google may ignore your wording."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "How to check and fix your titles in an afternoon"
      },
      {
        "type": "ol",
        "items": [
          "Export a list of your pages and their titles. A site crawler or the page source of each page will show the title element.",
          "Sort by title and look for exact duplicates, empty titles, and titles that only contain your practice name.",
          "Rewrite the highest-value pages first: home, your main services, each location and each provider.",
          "Open Google Search Console, go to Performance, and look at which pages get impressions but few clicks. Those are candidates for a clearer title.",
          "Publish the changes and give Google a few weeks to recrawl before judging them. Change one group of pages at a time so you can see what moved."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Titles work together with the snippet underneath. After you finish this pass, write matching descriptions using our [meta descriptions guide](/guides/meta-descriptions-that-get-clicks-for-a-medical-practice), and make sure each title agrees with the page's top heading, which our [Search Console guide](/guides/how-to-read-google-search-console-for-a-medical-practice-website) helps you monitor."
      }
