    "answer": "Once a month, open four things in Google Analytics 4: traffic acquisition to see where visitors came from, pages and screens to see what they read, engagement rate to see whether they stayed, and key events to see who called or submitted a form. Compare each to the prior month and write down what changed.",
    "author": "Gio LaRoche",
    "publishDate": "2026-10-07T09:00:00Z",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-07",
    "readMinutes": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Set a 20-minute monthly routine"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Google Analytics 4 can show hundreds of numbers, and most are noise for a medical practice. A short, repeatable routine works better than occasional deep dives. Pick the same day each month, set the date range to the previous full month, turn on the compare option for the month before, and look at four areas. If you have not yet installed GA4, start with our [GA4 setup guide](/guides/how-to-set-up-google-analytics-4-for-a-medical-practice)."
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Privacy comes first. Analytics tools can collect more than a medical site should, so confirm your setup follows our [HIPAA-safe tracking guide](/guides/hipaa-safe-tracking-for-a-medical-website) before you rely on any numbers. Nothing in a monthly review should require looking at an individual patient's activity."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Step 1: Where visitors came from"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Open Reports, then Acquisition, then Traffic acquisition. The default grouping sorts sessions by channel, such as Organic Search, Direct, Referral, Organic Social and Paid Search. For a practice, Organic Search is the channel your local SEO work should grow, and Direct often includes people typing your address or opening a saved link. Note whether Organic Search grew or shrank against last month, and treat a small change in a low-traffic month as noise rather than a trend."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Step 2: What they looked at"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Open Reports, then Engagement, then Pages and screens. Sort by views and look at the top ten pages. You are checking that the pages you want patients to see, such as services, providers, locations and the booking page, appear near the top. If the top pages are old blog posts and your main service pages are missing, that tells you where to add internal links or improve the page."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Step 3: Whether they stayed"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "GA4 reports engagement rate, which is the share of sessions that were engaged. Google's definition counts a session as engaged if it lasted longer than ten seconds, had a key event or had at least two page or screen views. Google explains this in its help on [engagement rate and bounce rate](https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/12195621). A falling engagement rate on an important page suggests the page is not meeting what visitors expected, though a single month is rarely conclusive."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "Step 4: Who took action"
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Key events are the actions you care about, such as clicking a phone number, submitting an appointment request or reaching a thank-you page. GA4 used to call these conversions. Open Reports, then Engagement, then Events, and check how many key events happened and which channel they came from. If you track calls and forms, our guide to [tracking phone calls](/guides/tracking-phone-calls-from-a-medical-website) shows what to set up. Count contact requests, never anything about a patient's health."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What to write down each month"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Measure (where to find it)",
          "What to note"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Sessions by channel (Traffic acquisition)",
            "Organic Search up, down or flat"
          ],
          [
            "Top pages (Pages and screens)",
            "Whether service and location pages are in the top ten"
          ],
          [
            "Engagement rate (Traffic acquisition or Pages)",
            "Any drop on a page that matters"
          ],
          [
            "Key events (Events)",
            "Calls and form submissions, and their channel"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "p",
        "text": "Keep these four lines in a simple log. After three or four months, the log shows direction far better than any single report. Pair it with your Search Console numbers, covered in our [Search Console guide](/guides/how-to-read-google-search-console-for-a-medical-practice-website), and with [return on marketing spend](/guides/how-to-measure-roi-on-medical-practice-marketing) when you compare against revenue."
      },
      {
        "type": "h2",
        "text": "What to ignore"
      },
      {
        "type": "ul",
        "items": [
          "Day-to-day swings. With the traffic a typical single-location practice gets, a bad Tuesday means little.",
          "Bounce rate on its own. GA4 reports it as the opposite of engagement rate, and it matters less than whether key events occurred.",
          "Vanity totals. Total sessions can rise because of bot traffic or a one-off referral without bringing any more patients.",
          "Demographic and device detail, unless you are making a specific design or advertising decision."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "How often should a practice look at Google Analytics?",
        "a": "Once a month is enough for most practices, using the previous full month compared with the month before. Daily checking encourages reacting to noise. If you launch a campaign or redesign, add a short check one and four weeks afterward."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a key event in GA4?",
        "a": "A key event is an action you mark as important, such as a phone-number click, a form submission or reaching a thank-you page. GA4 previously called these conversions. They show whether visits turn into contact attempts, so they matter more than raw traffic."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is engagement rate in GA4?",
        "a": "It is the share of sessions that were engaged. Google counts a session as engaged if it lasted longer than ten seconds, included a key event or had two or more page or screen views. It replaced bounce rate as the main measure of whether visitors stayed."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why do my GA4 numbers differ from Search Console?",
        "a": "They measure different things. Search Console counts impressions and clicks in Google Search, while GA4 counts sessions on your site from all sources, and privacy settings or blockers can prevent some visits from appearing in GA4. Expect differences and compare trends instead of exact totals."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is Google Analytics safe for a medical practice website?",
        "a": "It can be used carefully, but a medical site should avoid sending anything that could identify a patient or reveal a health condition, such as health details in page addresses or form fields. Review your setup with a compliance advisor, and see our HIPAA-safe tracking guide before relying on it."
      }
    ],
